# 5. Phase A — Back up and document the Windows computer

Complete this entire phase while still in Windows.

## 5.1 Back up the HDD

The Ubuntu installation will erase the approximately 2 TB HDD.

Move everything you need to:

* A separate external drive
* Another computer
* A trusted cloud location
* More than one location for irreplaceable files

Do not use the same HDD as its own backup.

Open a representative sample of copied files from the backup drive. Do not assume a successful-looking copy operation means the files are usable.

Verify:

* Documents open
* Photographs open
* Videos play
* ZIP archives open
* Important folders have plausible sizes
* Hidden files or application-data folders were included when necessary

## 5.2 Save the Windows BitLocker recovery key

Open **PowerShell as Administrator**.

Run:

```powershell
manage-bde -status
```

Look at the Windows `C:` drive.

If BitLocker or Device Encryption is enabled, run:

```powershell
manage-bde -protectors -get C:
```

Confirm that the recovery key is stored somewhere outside this computer.

Appropriate locations include:

* Your Microsoft account’s recovery-key page
* A printed paper stored securely
* A password manager
* An encrypted external backup

Do not store the only copy on the Windows SSD.

You do not ordinarily need to disable BitLocker when Ubuntu will be installed on a separate physical drive. You do need the recovery key because firmware or boot changes can occasionally cause Windows to request it.

## 5.3 Record the physical drives

In Administrator PowerShell, run:

```powershell
Get-Disk |
    Format-Table Number, FriendlyName, SerialNumber, PartitionStyle, OperationalStatus,
    @{Name="SizeGB";Expression={[math]::Round($_.Size/1GB,2)}} -AutoSize
```

Save the output:

```powershell
Get-Disk |
    Format-Table Number, FriendlyName, SerialNumber, PartitionStyle, OperationalStatus,
    @{Name="SizeGB";Expression={[math]::Round($_.Size/1GB,2)}} -AutoSize |
    Out-File "$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\physical-disks.txt"
```

Also run:

```powershell
Get-PhysicalDisk |
    Format-Table FriendlyName, SerialNumber, MediaType, HealthStatus,
    @{Name="SizeGB";Expression={[math]::Round($_.Size/1GB,2)}} -AutoSize
```

Write down:

```text
Windows SSD:
Manufacturer/model:
Serial number:
Approximate size:

Ubuntu HDD:
Manufacturer/model:
Serial number:
Approximate size:
```

Take a photograph of this information with your phone.

The Ubuntu installer may display decimal capacities differently. A marketed 1 TB disk may appear around 931 GiB, while a marketed 2 TB disk may appear around 1.82 TiB.

## 5.4 Record the Windows firmware mode

Press `Windows + R`.

Enter:

```text
msinfo32
```

Find:

```text
BIOS Mode
```

It should say:

```text
UEFI
```

Do not intentionally switch to Legacy or CSM mode.

Also note whether **Secure Boot State** is On or Off.

Secure Boot may remain enabled. Ubuntu supports Secure Boot, although installing the proprietary NVIDIA driver may require a one-time Machine Owner Key enrollment.

## 5.5 Disable Windows hibernation and Fast Startup

In Administrator PowerShell, run:

```powershell
powercfg /h off
```

This disables Windows hibernation and Fast Startup.

Fast Startup can leave Windows filesystems in a partially hibernated condition. Disabling it reduces dual-boot filesystem and shutdown problems.

You may re-enable hibernation later with:

```powershell
powercfg /h on
```

For a dual-boot machine, leaving it disabled is generally simpler.

## 5.6 Create a Windows recovery drive

Search Windows for:

```text
Create a recovery drive
```

Use a separate USB drive.

Follow the wizard and include system files when offered.

Label the USB:

```text
WINDOWS RECOVERY — DO NOT ERASE
```

Do not confuse this USB with the Ubuntu installer.

## 5.7 Create a final Windows restore point

Search for:

```text
Create a restore point
```

Select the Windows system drive and create a restore point named:

```text
Before Ubuntu AI Installation
```

A restore point does not replace a real backup, but it provides another recovery option.

