# 8. Phase D — Boot and inspect Ubuntu before installing

## 8.1 Open the motherboard boot menu

Turn the computer on.

Repeatedly press the motherboard boot-menu key.

Common keys include:

* `F12`
* `F11`
* `F8`
* `Esc`

Do not permanently change all firmware settings unless necessary.

Select the entry resembling:

```text
UEFI: [USB manufacturer or device name]
```

Do not choose a Legacy or non-UEFI USB entry.

## 8.2 Select Try Ubuntu first

At the Ubuntu menu, select:

```text
Try or Install Ubuntu
```

When presented with the choice, select:

```text
Try Ubuntu
```

This launches a live desktop without changing the disks.

## 8.3 Test essential hardware

Before installing, test:

* Keyboard
* Mouse
* Display resolution
* Ethernet or Wi-Fi
* Sound
* USB ports
* Basic desktop responsiveness

Open a Terminal with:

```text
Ctrl + Alt + T
```

Run:

```bash
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,MODEL,SERIAL,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS
```

Identify the approximately 2 TB HDD.

If the Windows SSD was disconnected, it should not appear.

If it remains connected, identify both drives by model, serial number, and size.

Also run:

```bash
sudo fdisk -l
```

Do not run any partitioning commands.

Record the Linux device names only for verification. Do not assume that `/dev/sda` always means HDD or that `/dev/nvme0n1` always means Windows.

## 8.4 Inspect HDD health

Installations run from a live USB may not include every diagnostic utility, so this can also be completed after installation. When available, open **Disks**, select the HDD, and review its SMART information.

Do not trust the HDD with important AI data if it reports:

* Failing SMART status
* Uncorrectable sectors
* Pending sectors
* Rapidly increasing reallocated sectors
* Frequent I/O errors

A used HDD can function while still being a poor long-term data store.

