# 11. Phase G — Update and secure the fresh Ubuntu installation

Open Terminal:

```text
Ctrl + Alt + T
```

## 11.1 Confirm the Ubuntu version

Run:

```bash
lsb_release -a
```

Expected release:

```text
Ubuntu 26.04 LTS
```

Also run:

```bash
uname -a
```

## 11.2 Install all updates

Run:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt full-upgrade -y
sudo apt autoremove --purge -y
sudo reboot
```

Enter the encryption passphrase after reboot.

Then open Terminal again.

## 11.3 Verify disk encryption

Run:

```bash
lsblk -f
```

You should see a structure containing a crypto layer, often labeled:

```text
crypto_LUKS
```

Also run:

```bash
sudo dmsetup ls --tree
```

You should see encrypted/LVM-backed devices beneath the physical HDD.

Check the root filesystem:

```bash
findmnt /
```

Do not post the full encryption metadata publicly because it can contain device identifiers.

## 11.4 Check free space

Run:

```bash
df -h /
```

The root filesystem should reflect most of the approximately 2 TB HDD.

## 11.5 Check HDD health

Install SMART tools:

```bash
sudo apt install -y smartmontools
```

Identify the HDD:

```bash
lsblk -d -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,SERIAL,ROTA
```

A rotational HDD generally shows:

```text
ROTA 1
```

Run the SMART report, replacing `sdX` with the HDD device:

```bash
sudo smartctl -a /dev/sdX
```

Do not blindly enter `/dev/sda`. Use the actual HDD identifier from `lsblk`.

Concerning values include:

```text
SMART overall-health self-assessment test result: FAILED
Current_Pending_Sector greater than 0
Offline_Uncorrectable greater than 0
Repeated read or I/O errors
```

If the drive appears unhealthy, stop building the AI database on it.

## 11.6 Install the NVIDIA driver

Check recommended drivers:

```bash
ubuntu-drivers devices
```

Install the recommended driver:

```bash
sudo ubuntu-drivers install
```

Reboot:

```bash
sudo reboot
```

If Secure Boot asks you to create or enroll a Machine Owner Key:

1. Create the temporary enrollment password when prompted.
2. Reboot.
3. Select **Enroll MOK**.
4. Select **Continue**.
5. Select **Yes**.
6. Enter the temporary password.
7. Reboot again.

After reboot, run:

