# 14. Phase J — Install Ollama

Ollama runs the local language and embedding models. Its official Linux installation supports a system service and NVIDIA GPU acceleration.

## 14.1 Download the official installation script

Do not pipe an unseen script directly into a root shell.

Run:

```bash
curl -fsSLo /tmp/ollama-install.sh https://ollama.com/install.sh
```

Inspect it:

```bash
less /tmp/ollama-install.sh
```

Press `q` to exit.

Install:

```bash
sudo sh /tmp/ollama-install.sh
```

Delete the temporary script:

```bash
rm -f /tmp/ollama-install.sh
```

## 14.2 Verify installation

Run:

```bash
ollama --version
sudo systemctl status ollama
```

## 14.3 Create the dedicated model directory

Run:

```bash
sudo install -d -o ollama -g ollama -m 0750 /srv/local-ai/ollama-models
```

## 14.4 Bind Ollama only to localhost

Create a systemd override:

```bash
sudo systemctl edit ollama
```

Enter:

```ini
[Service]
Environment="OLLAMA_HOST=127.0.0.1:11434"
Environment="OLLAMA_MODELS=/srv/local-ai/ollama-models"
Environment="OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=1"
Environment="OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=1"
Environment="OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=5m"
```

Save and exit.

Then run:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart ollama
sudo systemctl enable ollama
```

Verify:

```bash
sudo ss -ltnp | grep 11434
```

Expected:

```text
127.0.0.1:11434
```

Do not continue if it shows:

```text
0.0.0.0:11434
```

## 14.5 Verify the Ollama API

Run:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/tags
```

A new installation will return valid JSON, probably with an empty model list.

## 14.6 Pull the initial models

Install the retrieval model:

```bash
ollama pull embeddinggemma:latest
```

Install the practical everyday model:

```bash
ollama pull gemma4:e4b
```

Install the higher-quality model for testing:

```bash
ollama pull gemma4:12b
```

Gemma 4 provides E2B, E4B, 12B, 26B, and 31B variants. The E4B model is intended for edge deployment, while 12B and larger models target workstation use.

With 8 GB VRAM and 16 GB system RAM:

* E4B should be the initial everyday model.
* 12B may run partly in system RAM and may be slow.
* 26B and 31B should wait until the planned RAM upgrade and possibly a stronger GPU.
* The HDD will make initial model loading slower.
* Swap may prevent a crash but will not make an oversized model responsive.

## 14.7 Create a context-controlled everyday model

Create a Modelfile:

```bash
cat > ~/Modelfile.zach-fast <<'EOF'
FROM gemma4:e4b

PARAMETER num_ctx 16384
PARAMETER temperature 1.0
PARAMETER top_p 0.95
PARAMETER top_k 64
EOF
```

Create the model:

```bash
ollama create zach-fast -f ~/Modelfile.zach-fast
```

## 14.8 Create a higher-quality model

Create:

```bash
cat > ~/Modelfile.zach-deep <<'EOF'
FROM gemma4:12b

PARAMETER num_ctx 8192
PARAMETER temperature 1.0
PARAMETER top_p 0.95
PARAMETER top_k 64
EOF
```

Create it:

```bash
ollama create zach-deep -f ~/Modelfile.zach-deep
```

The smaller initial context on the 12B model reduces memory pressure.

## 14.9 Test the models

Run:

```bash
ollama run zach-fast
```

Enter:

```text
Briefly identify yourself and state that you are running locally.
```

Exit with:

```text
Ctrl + D
```

Then test:

```bash
ollama run zach-deep
```

While generating a response, open another terminal and run:

```bash
watch -n 1 nvidia-smi
```

Also run:

```bash
ollama ps
```

Record:

* GPU percentage
* System RAM usage
* Response speed
* Whether the model crashes or becomes unusably slow

Do not assume the larger model is automatically the right default. Quality matters, but a model that is too slow to use regularly will not serve as an effective assistant.

