# 15. Phase K — Install Open WebUI natively

Open WebUI provides:

* The browser chat interface
* Conversation history
* Authentication
* Knowledge bases
* Document retrieval
* Connection to Ollama
* Model presets
* File citations

It can operate locally and offline. Its official documentation supports native installation through Python 3.11 and `uv`.

We are using a native, restricted system service instead of Open WebUI Computer or an agent container.

## 15.1 Create the service user

Run:

```bash
sudo useradd \
    --system \
    --create-home \
    --home-dir /var/lib/aiweb \
    --shell /usr/sbin/nologin \
    aiweb
```

Add it to the controlled groups:

```bash
sudo usermod -aG ai-readers,ai-writers aiweb
```

Create application directories:

```bash
sudo install -d -o aiweb -g aiweb -m 0750 /opt/open-webui
sudo chown -R aiweb:aiweb /srv/local-ai/open-webui
sudo chmod -R 0750 /srv/local-ai/open-webui
```

## 15.2 Install `uv`

Download the official installer:

```bash
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh -o /tmp/uv-install.sh
```

Inspect it:

```bash
less /tmp/uv-install.sh
```

Install it for your user:

```bash
sh /tmp/uv-install.sh
```

Copy the binary to a system path:

```bash
sudo install -m 0755 "$HOME/.local/bin/uv" /usr/local/bin/uv
```

Verify:

```bash
/usr/local/bin/uv --version
```

Remove the installer:

```bash
rm -f /tmp/uv-install.sh
```

## 15.3 Install Python 3.11 for the service

Run:

```bash
sudo -u aiweb -H /usr/local/bin/uv python install 3.11
```

Create the virtual environment:

```bash
sudo -u aiweb -H /usr/local/bin/uv venv \
    --python 3.11 \
    /opt/open-webui/venv
```

Install Open WebUI:

```bash
sudo -u aiweb -H /usr/local/bin/uv pip install \
    --python /opt/open-webui/venv/bin/python \
    'open-webui[all]'
```

Verify:

```bash
sudo -u aiweb -H /opt/open-webui/venv/bin/open-webui --help
```

Record the installed version:

```bash
sudo -u aiweb -H /opt/open-webui/venv/bin/python \
    -m pip show open-webui
```

Save the version number in:

```text
/srv/local-ai/config/installed-versions.txt
```

For example:

```bash
{
    echo "Installed on: $(date --iso-8601=seconds)"
    ollama --version
    /opt/open-webui/venv/bin/python -m pip show open-webui | grep -E '^(Name|Version):'
    psql --version
    nvidia-smi --query-gpu=name,driver_version,memory.total --format=csv,noheader
} | sudo tee /srv/local-ai/config/installed-versions.txt
```

## 15.4 Generate the Open WebUI secret

Run:

```bash
WEBUI_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
```

Verify its length without displaying it:

```bash
echo "${#WEBUI_SECRET}"
```

Expected:

```text
64
```

## 15.5 Create the secret environment directory

Run:

```bash
sudo install -d -o root -g aiweb -m 0750 /etc/local-ai
```

## 15.6 Create the Open WebUI environment file

The following command expands the secret variables into the root-protected file.

Run:

```bash
sudo tee /etc/local-ai/open-webui.env >/dev/null <<EOF
DATA_DIR=/srv/local-ai/open-webui/data

DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://openwebui:${DB_PASS}@127.0.0.1:5432/openwebui

VECTOR_DB=pgvector
PGVECTOR_DB_URL=postgresql://openwebui:${DB_PASS}@127.0.0.1:5432/openwebui
PGVECTOR_CREATE_EXTENSION=false

ENABLE_OLLAMA_API=true
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
ENABLE_OPENAI_API=false

RAG_EMBEDDING_ENGINE=ollama
RAG_EMBEDDING_MODEL=embeddinggemma:latest
ENABLE_RAG_HYBRID_SEARCH=true
RAG_TOP_K=8

ENABLE_WEB_SEARCH=false
ENABLE_CODE_INTERPRETER=false
ENABLE_KB_EXEC=false

ENABLE_SIGNUP=true
ENABLE_PERSISTENT_CONFIG=false

OFFLINE_MODE=true
HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1
