# 21. Backup system

The encrypted HDD protects data when the computer is powered off. It does not protect against:

* HDD failure
* Accidental deletion
* Database corruption
* Bad software updates
* Theft while unlocked
* Ransomware
* User error

A backup stored only on the same HDD is not a real hardware-failure backup.

## 21.1 Create local PostgreSQL dumps

Create a root backup script:

```bash
sudo tee /usr/local/sbin/backup-local-ai-databases >/dev/null <<'EOF'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

BACKUP_DIR="/srv/local-ai/backups-local/postgres"
TIMESTAMP="$(date +%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S)"

install -d -m 0700 "$BACKUP_DIR"

sudo -u postgres pg_dump -Fc openwebui \
    > "$BACKUP_DIR/openwebui_${TIMESTAMP}.dump"

sudo -u postgres pg_dump -Fc personal_data \
    > "$BACKUP_DIR/personal_data_${TIMESTAMP}.dump"

find "$BACKUP_DIR" -type f -name '*.dump' -mtime +30 -delete
EOF
```

Make executable:

```bash
sudo chmod 0700 /usr/local/sbin/backup-local-ai-databases
```

Test:

```bash
sudo /usr/local/sbin/backup-local-ai-databases
```

List results:

```bash
sudo ls -lh /srv/local-ai/backups-local/postgres
```

## 21.2 Create a daily database-backup timer

Create the service:

```bash
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/local-ai-db-backup.service >/dev/null <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Back up local AI PostgreSQL databases

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/backup-local-ai-databases
EOF
```

Create the timer:

```bash
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/local-ai-db-backup.timer >/dev/null <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Daily local AI database backup

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 03:15:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=15m

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
EOF
```

Enable:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now local-ai-db-backup.timer
```

Verify:

```bash
systemctl list-timers | grep local-ai
```

## 21.3 Use an encrypted external backup repository

Install Restic:

```bash
sudo apt install -y restic
```

Connect an external backup drive.

Identify it:

```bash
lsblk -f
```

Mount it through the Files application.

Its path may resemble:

```text
/media/yourusername/AI_BACKUP
```

Create a Restic password and store it in your password manager. Losing this password means losing access to the backup.

Initialize the repository:

```bash
restic -r /media/$USER/AI_BACKUP/local-ai-restic init
```

Back up:

```bash
sudo /usr/local/sbin/backup-local-ai-databases
```

Then:

```bash
restic -r /media/$USER/AI_BACKUP/local-ai-restic backup \
    /srv/local-ai/vault \
    /srv/local-ai/imports \
    /srv/local-ai/proposed \
    /srv/local-ai/reports \
    /srv/local-ai/config \
    /srv/local-ai/backups-local/postgres
```

Do not back up the downloaded Ollama model files initially. They are large and can be downloaded again.

Do back up:

* Obsidian vault
* Approved and proposed memory
* PostgreSQL dumps
* System-design records
* Custom Modelfiles
* Report outputs
* Nonsecret configuration records

The root-only `/etc/local-ai/open-webui.env` contains secrets. Back it up only into an encrypted repository with controlled access.

## 21.4 Test a restore

Create a temporary restore directory:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/restic-restore-test
```

List snapshots:

```bash
restic -r /media/$USER/AI_BACKUP/local-ai-restic snapshots
```

Restore one snapshot:

```bash
restic -r /media/$USER/AI_BACKUP/local-ai-restic restore latest \
    --target ~/restic-restore-test
```

Open at least one restored Markdown file.

Delete the test only after verifying it:

```bash
rm -rf ~/restic-restore-test
```

A backup has not been validated until a restore has succeeded.

